# Shell scripts must keep LF endings whatever the checkout platform.
#
# Git converts LF to CRLF on checkout wherever `core.autocrlf=true`, which is
# the Windows default. The blobs here are LF, so this costs nothing on Linux --
# but on a Windows checkout every one of these scripts fails on its second line
# with `set: pipefail: invalid option name`, because the shell reads the
# trailing carriage return as part of the option name. CI runs on Linux and
# never sees any of it, so the failure looks like a broken checkout rather
# than a missing attribute -- and the message points nowhere near the cause.
*.sh text eol=lf

# PowerShell reads LF natively on both 5.1 and 7+; pinning keeps the blob
# canonical instead of letting autocrlf churn it on Windows checkouts.
*.ps1 text eol=lf

# Generated file compared byte-identically by manifest_coverage; same CRLF
# checkout hazard as the scripts above.
docs/rpc-reference.md text eol=lf

# Corpus seeds are binary fuzz inputs; even byte-identical
# text-looking seeds must survive checkout unconverted.
fuzz/corpus/** -text

# Binary fixtures embedded with `include_bytes!`. A fixture without a NUL byte
# is detected as text, so autocrlf inserts a CR before every 0x0a byte and the
# tests reading it fail on Windows checkouts only (addrman-historical-v2.dat,
# addrman-historical-v4-anchors.dat).
*.dat -text
*.raw -text

# The corpus manifest is generated JSON — text that must stay LF even
# though the directory above it is pinned binary.
fuzz/corpus/manifest.json text eol=lf
